## Where the green ends and the desert begins.

On Luxor's West Bank, the river's gift has an edge you can see. Sugar cane and clover run right up to a line in the ground, and beyond it there is only sand and the bare Theban hills, the hills that hide the Valley of the Kings.

We ride out to that line at the cool end of the day, along field paths and past village houses, with the light going gold. It is a private ride, just your group and a guide from the stable, at whatever pace suits you.

## The horses come first.

A ride is only as good as the horse beneath you, and the way that horse is kept. We only ride with a stable your concierge has visited and checked in person, and we seek the guidance of Animal Care in Egypt (ACE), Luxor's charity equine hospital, on which stables keep their horses well.

Every ride follows the same terms: a rider weight limit of about 90 kg, rest with water and shade between rides, no riding in the midday heat, and quiet tracks rather than tarmac. We never book carriage horses.

## For beginners and riders alike.

You do not need to have ridden before. Each rider is matched to a calm horse and fitted with a helmet, and the ride is mainly at a walk. Children ride with an adult, following the stable's guidance.

It fits naturally after a morning at the tombs and temples on Luxor's West Bank, with a long rest in the middle of the day. Your concierge composes the rest around it.
